WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.425
Logistic Regression: The Classification Ninja What is Logistic Regression? Confusing name alert! Despite being called "regression," logistic regression is actually used for classification - predicting which category something belongs to. Think: Will this email be spam? Will the customer churn? Will the student pass? These are yes/no questions, and logistic regression answers them with probabilities. Why the Name? It's called "regression" because it predicts a continuous value (probability between 0 and 1). We then threshold that probability to make a classification.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:51.275
Blame the statisticians who named it in the 1800s! How Does It Actually Work? Step 1: Calculate a Linear Score Just like linear regression, we compute a weighted sum: This z can be any number from -∞ to +∞. Not great for probabilities yet... Step 2: Squish Through the Sigmoid Here's where the magic happens. The sigmoid function takes ANY number and squishes it into the range 0-1: Aha! Here's the cool part... The sigmoid has a beautiful property: negative z → probability close to 0, positive z → probability close to 1, and z = 0 → exactly 50/50.

00:00:51.275 --> 00:01:17.925
The more extreme the z, the more confident the prediction! Step 3: Make a Decision By default, if probability > 0.5, predict class 1. Otherwise, predict class 0. Simple as that! Step 4: Learn from Mistakes The model uses gradient descent to adjust weights w and bias b, minimizing the log loss (also called binary cross-entropy): Intuition Log loss severely punishes confident wrong predictions. If you're 99% sure something is class 1 but it's actually class 0? That's a HUGE penalty. Being humble about uncertain predictions is rewarded. Worked Example with Numbers The Decision Boundary

00:01:17.925 --> 00:01:45.025
The decision boundary is where the probability = 0.5 (which means z = 0). For our example: Study 5+ hours and you're predicted to pass. Less than 5? Predicted to fail. The decision boundary is at x = 5 hours. The Knobs and Dials You Can Tweak Inverse of regularization strength (smaller = more regularization) Confusing! Higher C = LESS regularization. Yes, everyone gets this backwards. Regularization type: 'l1', 'l2', 'elasticnet', or 'none' L1 can zero out features (feature selection). L2 shrinks them. Optimization algorithm lbfgs is great for small datasets. saga handles L1 and large data.

00:01:45.025 --> 00:02:11.800
Maximum iterations for solver Increase if you get convergence warnings Weight for each class or 'balanced' to auto-adjust Use 'balanced' for imbalanced datasets (e.g., 95% vs 5% classes) Common Confusion The C parameter is INVERSE regularization strength. Higher C = LESS regularization = model is more free to fit the data. This is backwards from what most people expect. You've been warned! Where You'll Actually Use This Spam detection: Is this email spam or not? Medical diagnosis: Does the patient have the disease? Credit scoring: Will this person default on the loan? Click prediction:

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:38.875
Will the user click this ad? Churn prediction: Will this customer cancel their subscription? Google Uses This Google uses logistic regression (among many other techniques) for ad click prediction. With billions of ad impressions, even a tiny improvement in prediction accuracy is worth millions of dollars. Let's Build Something Real-World Example: Spam Detection Level Up: From Good to Great Handling Imbalanced Data If 95% of your data is class 0 and only 5% is class 1, your model might just predict 0 for everything and get 95% accuracy. That's useless!

00:02:38.875 --> 00:03:05.975
Use class_weight='balanced' to automatically adjust for imbalance Oversample the minority class using SMOTE Adjust the threshold - maybe 0.3 is better than 0.5 for your case Beyond Binary Classification Logistic regression can handle multiple classes too! Options: One-vs-Rest (OvR): Train one classifier per class Softmax (Multinomial): Single model that outputs probabilities for all classes at once Pro Tip Don't judge your classifier by accuracy alone! For imbalanced data, look at precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC. A model with 95% accuracy might be missing every important case.

00:03:05.975 --> 00:03:19.800
